        content: "**Structure of DNA**\n\nDNA is a double helix — often described as a \"twisted ladder.\"\n\n**Nucleotide Structure:**\nEach DNA nucleotide consists of three components:\n1. **Pentose Sugar** — deoxyribose (a 5-carbon sugar).\n2. **Phosphate Group** — links sugars together via phosphodiester bonds.\n3. **Nitrogenous Base** — one of four bases: Adenine (A), Thymine (T), Guanine (G), Cytosine (C).\n\n**The Watson-Crick Model:**\n- Two antiparallel strands wind around each other.\n- Sugar-phosphate backbone forms the uprights of the ladder.\n- Complementary base pairing forms the rungs: **A pairs with T** (2 hydrogen bonds), **G pairs with C** (3 hydrogen bonds).\n- The strands are antiparallel — one runs 5'→3', the other 3'→5'.\n- Major and minor grooves allow proteins to access and bind to the DNA.",
