        id: "bio-2-s3t1-content-4",
        type: "info",
        content: "**Honeybee (Apis species)**\n\nHoneybees are highly social insects that live in colonies.\n\n**Colony Structure (Three Castes):**\n1. **Queen**: Only fertile female; lays up to 2,000 eggs per day.\n2. **Workers**: Sterile females; perform all colony tasks (foraging, nursing, cleaning, guarding).\n3. **Drones**: Males; mate with the queen, then die.\n\n**Distinctive Features:**\n- Hairy body (helps collect pollen).\n- Pollen baskets on hind legs.\n- Stinger at the end of the abdomen (barbed; workers die after stinging).\n- Compound eyes and three simple ocelli.\n\n**Economic Importance:**\n- **Pollination**: Essential for crop production (cocoa, fruits, vegetables).\n- **Honey**: Food and medicinal product.\n- **Beeswax**: Used in cosmetics, candles, and polishes.\n- **Propolis**: A resinous substance with antimicrobial properties.\n- **Royal Jelly**: Used in health supplements.\n\n**Threats:** Colony Collapse Disorder (CCD), pesticides, habitat loss, and diseases.",
